The comment `@@@ The bit at the specified index is toggled to the opposite of its previous value.` indicates that after the `flip` operation, the value of the bit at `bitIndex` should be the logical negation of its value before the operation.

To express this in an assert statement:
- We need to compare the current value of the bit at `bitIndex` (after flipping) with the old value (before flipping).
- The new value should be the opposite (`!`) of the old value.

Using `\old(expression)` to capture the value before the method execution and `this.get(bitIndex)` to observe the current bit value, the assertion can be written accordingly.

<code>assert this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));</code>
--------------------
Let's analyze the comment starting with "@@@":

> // @@@ The bit at the specified index is toggled to the opposite of its previous value.

This means that after the `flip(int bitIndex)` operation, the value of the bit at position `bitIndex` should be the logical negation of what it was before the operation.

To express this in an assert statement:
- We need to refer to the value of the bit before the operation: `\old(this.get(bitIndex))`
- After the operation, `this.get(bitIndex)` should be the opposite (i.e., `!` of the old value).

So the assertion is:
```java
assert this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));
```

This precisely captures that the bit at `bitIndex` has been flipped (toggled) to the opposite of its previous value.

<code>assert this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));</code>
